                  "width": 1440,
                  "layout": "vertical",
                  "gap": 4,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "Ez6gS",
                      "name": "Caption title",
                      "fill": "$fg",
                      "content": "F5 · PR full page · the thread",
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 15,
                      "fontWeight": "500"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "s4L7N6",
                      "name": "Caption note",
                      "fill": "$fg-2",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "The thread under an issue or a pull request, GitHub's timeline made calmer: one rail runs down it, everything that happened sits on it as a one-line event, and what people wrote sits beside it in a card with its own header, so two comments never run together. A push lists its commits under it (three, then “Show N more”). A review is one card: the verdict, the summary, and each code thread with the lines it is about; resolved threads fold to one line. A long middle folds to “N earlier events”, and the line marks where the new ones start. Drawn whole, from the description down: the summary column stays pinned beside it, so where the pull request stands, its reviews and checks stay in view while the thread scrolls (F18).\n\nThe fold: a thread folds once it has more than 20 items (events, pushes, comments, reviews; the description and the composer don't count). The first 2 items stay above the fold, and everything stays in time order: what GitHub hasn't given yet, of the comments or of the events, folds with the middle, so nothing older stands below the fold or newer above it. Expanding reads the middle and puts it in place. Below it stays everything from the earliest of: the 5th item from the end, the first item new since you looked, and the oldest review or code comment with a thread still unresolved. If that would leave fewer than 6 items to fold, nothing folds. Here: 2 above, 18 folded, 7 below, starting at sofiab's review, whose thread is unresolved.\n\nNew since you looked: counted from the last time you had this item open, in any container, kept per item on this device (a rebuildable cache: on a new device nothing is marked). Your own comments, reviews and events count as looking, so they are never new. The line sits before the first item by someone else after that moment and says how many there are; it stays until you leave the item. A line with no person (CI, a Status change made by a project workflow) counts as someone else's. Here the viewer is mlaine: their request and comment count as having looked, so only dkim's comment after them is new.\n\nR: when a code thread is focused (moved to with J/K, or clicked), R opens that thread's reply box; otherwise R scrolls to the composer at the end and focuses it, as “Comment” in the command menu does. Esc leaves the editor (in a reply box, Esc is Cancel).",
                      "lineHeight": 1.45,
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 13,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                },
